{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>From day to day.</b> This teaches that the nullification of vows takes effect from the beginning of the day until its end — like the day spoken of in the work of Creation and in 'it and its following night,' where the day follows the night that has passed, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 1:5): ״ויהי ערב ויהי בקר יום אחד״ — and it is not from hour to hour. Therefore if she vowed at night she has the time for nullification all that night and all the following day, and if she vowed by day she has no time for nullification except until sunset. Consequently, a husband may nullify his wife's vows on the Sabbath even vows that are not for the need of the Sabbath, although it resembles adjudicating a case — for if not now, when? But a sage does not release vows on the Sabbath except those that are for the need of the Sabbath, for a sage, by means of remorse, releases it for the next day, for another day, and not for this day. And the time for nullification is not on the day she vowed alone, but on the day her father hears and on the day her husband hears, even after many days from when she vowed. And if her husband upheld her vow from when he heard of it and later nullified it for her and she relied upon him, he bears the punishment in her stead; and concerning this it is said here: ״ואם הפר יפר אותם אחרי שמעו ונשא עונה״ — 'But if he makes them void after he has heard, he bears her iniquity.' From here we learn that one who causes a stumbling block to his fellow enters into his place for all his punishments." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> It appears that this woman acted inadvertently or was misled, for the verse speaks of a husband who heard and the wife does not know of it, and after the day of his hearing he nullified it and said to her that it was the day of his hearing. And the verse teaches us two things: that the husband bears the iniquity as though he himself had vowed and violated his word, and that she is exempt and bears no punishment of inadvertences at all. But if the wife knows that he did not nullify it on the day of his hearing — being knowledgeable in this law — then she is liable, and the husband too is not the cause of the stumbling block, but his punishment is like one who could have protested and did not protest. And the verse mentioned this regarding the husband, and the same law applies to the father, but the verse spoke of the prevailing case, for the father will guard against this out of love for his daughter, whereas the husband may perhaps hate her and scheme to bring guilt upon her — thus far his words, of blessed memory.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:14.164783803Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}